// **A `Duration` is `Display` but not `Comparable`, and the checker says so.**
//
// A calendar span has no total order without a reference date — `PT1M` against `P1D` depends on
// which day — so `ExternValue::cmp_value` answers `None` and the declaration withholds `Comparable`.
// The negative half of `datetime_ordering_and_display.noe`: without this case, adding the trait to
// `Duration` for symmetry would type-check `a < b` and then fail at run time in both backends, which
// is exactly the failure a declaration is supposed to prevent.
//
// The **bound** is what makes this case discriminating, and the bare `a < b` below is not enough on
// its own: a runtime "cannot compare" abort raises the same `E0007` at the same span a static
// rejection does, so a `Duration` that wrongly declared `Comparable` satisfies an expectation naming
// only that code and position. An **E0025** at a `T: Comparable` call site has no runtime analogue —
// a type that declared the trait would satisfy the bound and the error would simply not appear.
// expect: error E0025 at 24:6
// expect: error E0007 at 25:6
// expect: exit 1
use std.{datetime}

fn smallest<T: Comparable>(xs: List<T>): ?T { return xs.min(); }

t = datetime.from_unix_ms(0);
a = t.diff(t.add(datetime.hours(1)));
b = t.diff(t.add(datetime.hours(2)));
echo smallest([a, b]);
echo a < b;
